description:
pkix-lint/src/lib.rs:1648 (LintProfile trait) + ~1661 (lint_runner method) + 1716 (check_shape) — The LintProfile trait has:

  pub trait LintProfile: Profile {
      fn lint_runner(&self) -> LintRunner;
  }

And the rustdoc says (paraphrased): "Implementors should document whether this method caches the runner or allocates fresh on each call."

This pushes the contract onto every implementor. A caller writing generic code over `dyn LintProfile` cannot rely on any default behavior — they don't know whether `profile.lint_runner()` is cheap (cached) or expensive (fresh allocation).

CALLER IMPACT:

check_shape (lib.rs:1716) calls profile.lint_runner() on EVERY invocation:

  pub fn check_shape(profile: &dyn LintProfile, cert: &Certificate, ...) -> Vec<Finding> {
      let runner = profile.lint_runner();   // fresh per call — implementor's choice
      runner.run_cert(cert, ...)
  }

A caller running check_shape in a tight loop over many certs incurs:
- Per-cert LintRunner::new() call (which has an O(n log n) duplicate-id check, see lib.rs:1181's # Panics doc).
- Per-cert clone of every Box<dyn Lint> (via LintClone::clone_box if the implementor builds the runner from owned lints).
- Per-cert Vec allocation for the lint set.

For a profile with 30 lints, this is ~30 Box::clone() per cert call. At 1000 certs/sec batch validation (per AGENTS.md non-negotiable #6's batch-evaluation requirement), that's 30000 Box::clone() / sec for the lint set, before any actual lint work runs.

The implementor cannot fix this within the current trait shape because lint_runner returns owned LintRunner. There is no way to return a cached &LintRunner from an impl.

REMEDIATION OPTIONS:

1. PIN THE CONTRACT IN THE TRAIT RUSTDOC:
   /// # Contract
   /// Implementors SHOULD allocate fresh on each call. Callers MUST cache
   /// the returned LintRunner if they invoke it repeatedly; the caller side
   /// is the appropriate place for the cache.

   Documentation-only. Doesn't address the underlying inefficiency but documents the contract.

2. CHANGE THE RETURN TYPE TO Cow<'_, LintRunner>:
   fn lint_runner(&self) -> Cow<'_, LintRunner>;

   - Implementors with cached state can return Cow::Borrowed.
   - Implementors building fresh can return Cow::Owned.
   - check_shape uses the runner via &*runner; works either way.
   - Caller batch code can store the Cow and pay the cost once.

3. SEPARATE THE CONCERNS:
   fn lint_set(&self) -> &[Box<dyn Lint>];
   fn bundle_version(&self) -> &str { "" }

   And let callers build LintRunner themselves once. Pushes the runner-construction responsibility caller-side, which is where batching wants it anyway.

Option 2 is the highest-flexibility / lowest-friction choice. Option 3 is the most architecturally clean.

PRE-1.0: Either is a breaking signature change and is free now. Post-1.0, only Option 1 remains.

RELATED: AGENTS.md non-negotiable #6 — "Batch APIs where setup cost is non-trivial. Policy-adapter crates with subprocess overhead MUST expose batch APIs..." — applies to LintProfile by analogy. The per-cert lint_runner() call is non-trivial setup repeated unnecessarily.

The current state — delegating the contract to implementors — means pkix-lint cannot guarantee batch-friendliness for any LintProfile-using code. That violates non-negotiable #6 unless the caller knows to cache.
